**Read:** [Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA](../../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ), verification conditions and intended use; [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), process modelling and experimental design. Revisit the facility balances.

A qualification domain includes the combinations of loads, states and environments for which a claim is supported. Testing one factor at a time can miss interactions: a door event may matter differently under two airflow states. The most adverse combination cannot always be found by varying each factor separately.

For a benign model y = 50 + 3A + 2B + 4AB with A and B each taking −1 or +1, the four responses are 49, 47, 45 and 59 for (−1,−1), (+1,−1), (−1,+1), (+1,+1). The effect of changing A depends on B. Testing only domain corners also needs a physical argument: f(x) = x(1−x) on [0,1] has zero corner values and an interior maximum of 0.25.

**Work:** Reconstruct these examples and design observations that separate main effects from interaction and curvature. Explain why a chosen condition challenges the physical requirement. State what remains extrapolation and how a changed product or loading pattern would affect the supported domain.

